      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Pterocephalus hookeri (C. B. Clarke) Hock., a traditional Tibetan herbal medicine rich in glycosides, has been used to treat several diseases including rheumatoid arthritis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To evaluate the anti-arthritic activity of total glycosides from P. hookeri, and its possible mechanisms of action."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MATERIALS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Anti-arthritic activity of total glycosides from P. hookeri (oral administration for 30 days at 14-56\u2009mg/kg) was evaluated using paw swelling, arthritis scores and histopathological measurement in adjuvant-induced arthritis (AA) Sprague-Dawley rats. The NF-κB p65 expression in synovial tissues, and serum superoxide dismutase (SOD) activity, malondialdehyde (MDA) and nitric oxide (NO) levels was measured in AA rats, respectively. Further assessment of anti-inflammatory and analgesic activities of these glycosides were carried out using inflammation and hyperalgesia models induced by xylene, carrageenan, agar and acetic acid, respectively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Total glycosides (56\u2009mg/kg) decreased the paw swelling (38.0%, p\u2009<\u20090.01), arthritis scores (25.3%, p\u2009<\u20090.01) and synovial inflammation in AA rats. The glycosides significantly (p\u2009<\u20090.05-0.01) attenuated the inflammation induced by xylene, carrageenan, acetic acid and agar, increased the pain threshold in acetic acid-induced writhing in mice and mechanical stimuli-induced hyperalgia in AA rats. The glycosides (14, 28, 56\u2009mg/kg) also suppressed the NF-κB p65 expression (33.1-78.2%, p\u2009<\u20090.05-0.01), reduced MDA (21.3-35.9%, p\u2009<\u20090.01) and NO (20.3-32.4%, p\u2009<\u20090.05-0.01) levels, respectively, enhanced the SOD activity (7.8%, p\u2009<\u20090.05) at 56\u2009mg/kg in AA rats."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Our findings confirmed the anti-arthritic property of the total glycosides from P. hookeri, which may be attributed to its inhibition on NF-κB signalling and oxidative stress."
        }
      ]},
